# Demo Player Lesson Authoring Rules

These rules apply whenever creating or modifying lesson files under
`src/features/demo-player/lessons/`.

---

## Done checklist (merge gate) — 5 items only

**Before marking a lesson task complete or merging lesson-only work**, complete the
five-item checklist in [`docs/guides/demo-lesson-done-checklist.md`](../../docs/guides/demo-lesson-done-checklist.md).

That checklist replaces the old implicit bar (~30 checks): per-step unit tests on
wrappers, concept/diagram assertions, and >90% coverage on thin lesson files.

| Layer | Rule |
|---|---|
| **Authoring sections below (§1–11)** | How to write a *good* lesson |
| **Done checklist (5 items)** | Merge gate — when the lesson is *shippable* |
| **Lesson helpers + demo player core** | Full product test/coverage bar (not relaxed) |

Copy into PR descriptions:

```text
Demo lesson checklist:
[ ] 1 manual 1× run + rapid Next
[ ] 2 E2E smoke
[ ] 3 helper unit tests (if helpers changed)
[ ] 4 selectors + demo tab
[ ] 5 tsc + scoped vitest
```

---

## 1. Write for a Human Viewer — Not the Machine

Demos are watched by **people** reading narration and glancing at the UI. The runner,
E2E tests, and `preAction` guards only care that selectors exist — **the viewer
cares that they can see what changed.**

**Default mindset:** If a viewer would say “wait, what just happened?”, the step is
too fast or highlighting the wrong thing.

### What the viewer needs on every step

1. **Read** — narration + title (automatic reading pause before action)
2. **Look** — spotlight on the **one** UI element this step is about (`highlight`)
3. **Watch** — a visible action (`ctx.click` / `ctx.fill`) with ripple, paced so the
   eye can follow
4. **Confirm** — pause on the **outcome** (badge, panel, response, badge) before Next

`preAction` is **invisible** — never put the only “important moment” there. If the
user must *see* it, it belongs in `action()` (with delays) or in its own step.

### Spotlight (`highlight`) must match the lesson beat

- `highlight` = where the viewer should look **while reading** and **while the action runs**
- If the step clicks **Introspect**, highlight `GQL.INTROSPECT_BTN` — not a unrelated panel
- If the step shows **Metadata request headers**, highlight that panel — narration and
  ring must agree
- **One focal target per step.** Split the step if two things both need attention

Misaligned spotlight (narration says “watch the schema badge” but ring is on the
endpoint field) is a common reason demos feel broken even when E2E passes.

### Action speed — minimum delays between visible beats

Every **visible** sub-action inside `action()` needs `ctx.delay()` after it. The
machine finishes in milliseconds; the viewer needs **hundreds of ms to a second**
to relocate their eyes and recognize the change.

| Event | Minimum delay |
|---|---|
| Tab / panel switch | `800ms` |
| Form opens (empty) | `600ms` |
| Filled input / select change | `400–500ms` |
| Auto-generated content (Generate, Auto-detect) | `1500–2000ms` |
| Save / submit — result card appears | `700ms` |
| **Outcome the step is teaching** (badge, TLS indicator, history row) | `800–1200ms` |

A step that runs 5 visible sub-actions in under 1 second total is **too fast** for a
human viewer. When in doubt, **add more delay** — especially after the **payoff**
(the green schema badge, the mTLS indicator, the cURL menu).

### Pattern: click → waitFor → delay → interact

After a click that opens UI, **never** fill/select immediately:

```typescript
await ctx.click(GQL.TLS_CONFIGURE);
await ctx.waitFor(GQL.TLS_BODY);   // React rendered the modal
await ctx.delay(600);              // viewer sees empty form
await ctx.fill(GQL.TLS_CA_CERT, pem);
await ctx.delay(800);              // viewer sees pasted cert / indicator update
```

### Validate at 1× speed as a human

Before marking done: play the lesson at **1×** (not 2×). If **you** miss a change,
slow down that step — do not rely on E2E or unit tests to prove watchability.

---

## 2. One Concept Per Step

Split into separate steps when any of the following is true:

- There are two conceptually distinct things to explain (e.g. "enable the toggle"
  vs. "fill the form")
- The action sequence takes more than ~5–6 seconds of wall time
- The user needs to understand part A before part B makes sense

Combining "enable validation + open form + fill all fields + save" into one step is
too dense. A viewer who blinks will miss the point.

---

## 3. Always Demonstrate the "Power User" Feature

If a panel has a **Generate**, **Auto-detect**, **Smart fill**, or **Import** button
that does non-obvious magic, the demo **MUST** show it — even if the final result
replaces what it produces.

- Click the button with a visible ripple (use `ctx.click()`)
- Insert a `1500–2000ms` delay immediately after so the viewer can read the output
- Only then modify / refine the result

Skipping these buttons leaves viewers unaware the feature exists.

---

## 4. `preAction` Guards for Every Stateful Step

Any step that relies on state produced by a previous step (a card existing, a toggle
being on, a form already filled) **MUST** have a `preAction` that recreates that
state quietly when it is missing.

This covers:
- User clicking **Next** during the reading pause (action of the prior step never ran)
- User restarting the lesson mid-way
- Steps being replayed individually

Use `buildQuietContext` / `ctx` without ripple in `preAction` — these operations
should be invisible to the viewer.

**Also close stray overlays in `preAction`** when the prior step may have left them
open (user clicked Next early, or a prior run failed mid-step). See section 5.

```typescript
preAction: async (ctx) => {
  if (!document.querySelector(WS.SCHEMA_CARD)) {
    // recreate schema silently ...
  }
},
```

---

## 5. Close Modals and Overlays Before the Step Ends

A step that **opens** a modal, drawer, or full-screen panel must **close** it before
the viewer advances — unless the **next** step explicitly teaches something inside
that overlay.

**Why this breaks demos:** An open TLS / env / profile / workflow config modal covers
the canvas the next step spotlights. The viewer sees the wrong UI; the next step's
`highlight` may point at something hidden behind the overlay; E2E may still pass if
selectors exist in the DOM.

### Rule: open → configure → pause on outcome → **close** → Next

The last beats of a configuring step should be:

1. Viewer sees the result (indicator badge, saved state, preview) — `delay(800–1200ms)`
2. **Visible Close** (`ctx.click(GQL.TLS_CLOSE)` or domain equivalent) — ripple so the
   viewer sees the panel dismiss
3. `delay(700ms)` — confirm the main studio is visible again
4. Step ends; `verify` should target something on the **main** surface, not inside the modal

```typescript
// ✅ End of TLS configure step — viewer sees close
await ctx.delay(1200);                    // read indicator / pasted cert
await ctx.click(GQL.TLS_CLOSE);
await ctx.delay(700);

// ❌ Step ends with GQL.TLS_BODY still in DOM — next step spotlights schema behind modal
await ctx.fill(GQL.TLS_CA_CERT, pem);
// forgot close — advance to next step
```

### When the next step needs the modal closed

| Opened in action | Close with | Quiet close helper (preAction) |
|---|---|---|
| TLS Configure | `GQL.TLS_CLOSE` or `GQL.TLS_SAVE` | `closeGqlTlsModal` in lesson-https-tls helpers |
| Auth panel | switch to Variables tab | `closeAuthPanelQuiet` in core |
| Env / Profile modal | Cancel or Close | lesson-specific preIntro / cleanup |
| Workflow node config | Close in modal footer | `closeWfConfigModalQuiet` in workflow lesson helpers |
| History / Collections drawer | activity tab toggle | setup/cleanup or next step preAction |

Reuse existing `*Quiet` close helpers — do not copy-paste `querySelector?.remove()` in
each lesson unless tests require DOM stubs.

### Defensive belt: next step `preAction`

Even when the current step closes correctly, **the next step's `preAction`** should
idempotently close known overlays if still present (user skipped reading and missed
the close action):

```typescript
preAction: async (ctx) => {
  await ensureTlsCaConfigured(ctx);   // state
  if (document.querySelector(GQL.TLS_BODY)) {
    await closeGqlTlsModal(ctx, { visible: false }); // quiet — no second ripple tour
  }
},
```

### `verify` must match a visible main UI

If `verify` is `GQL.SCHEMA_BADGE_OK`, the schema badge must be visible **without** a
modal open. If the step's job was configuring a modal, either close first then verify
the badge, or set `verify` to the modal outcome **and** add a follow-up step that closes.

---

## 6. The React Async Render Timing Rule

After clicking a button that triggers a **React state change** (opening an editor
form, expanding a panel, etc.), **never** call `ctx.fill()` or `ctx.selectOption()`
immediately. The DOM update is async and the target element may not exist yet.

**Always** insert `await ctx.waitFor(CHILD_SELECTOR)` to wait for the new DOM to
render before interacting with it:

```typescript
// ✅ Correct
await ctx.click(WS.SCHEMA_ADD_BTN);
await ctx.waitFor(WS.SCHEMA_NAME_INPUT);   // wait for React to render the form
await ctx.delay(600);                       // pause so the viewer sees the empty form
await ctx.fill(WS.SCHEMA_NAME_INPUT, 'My Schema');

// ❌ Wrong — fill() is a silent no-op if the input doesn't exist yet
await ctx.click(WS.SCHEMA_ADD_BTN);
await ctx.fill(WS.SCHEMA_NAME_INPUT, 'My Schema');
```

The pattern is always: **click → waitFor(child) → delay(pause) → interact**.

---

## 7. Descriptions Must Explain WHY, Not Just WHAT

The `description` field is the narration a real human reads. It must answer:
- Why does this feature exist?
- What will they see on screen?
- What should they pay attention to?

```
// ❌ Too sparse — just describes the click
'The Schema tab lets you add a schema. Click + Add and fill it in.'

// ✅ Educational — explains context, intent, and what to watch for
'Click **+ Add** to open the schema editor. Hit **Generate** to auto-build a ' +
'schema from your existing received messages — no JSON expertise needed. ' +
'Then name it, set direction to **Both** (validate sent AND received), and ' +
'replace the generated schema with a strict version...'
```

Use `**bold**` for button names and important terms. Use backticks for field names
and JSON keys.

---

## 8. Keep Selectors in `src/shared/selectors.ts`

All `data-testid` selector strings used in lesson actions **must** be defined as
named constants in the `WS` / `SSE` / etc. namespace in `src/shared/selectors.ts`.

- Never inline a raw `'[data-testid="..."]'` string inside a lesson file
- Add the constant before writing the lesson step
- Use descriptive names: `SCHEMA_GENERATE_BTN`, not `GENERATE`

---

## 9. Tests for Lessons (Lightweight)

When restructuring steps, keep **E2E smoke** and **helper tests** in sync — not
exhaustive wrapper unit tests.

**Required when steps change:**
- E2E spec walks the new step count (done checklist item 2)
- Update `estimatedMinutes` if step count changed materially (section 9)

**Required when helpers change:**
- Unit tests for new/changed logic in `*-lesson-helpers/**`, `gql-demo-tab`, setup/cleanup, session flags
- Happy path + guard/skip path for stateful helpers

**Optional (do not block merge):**
- Unit tests asserting concept body, diagram SVG, key-term counts
- Per-step `action()` tests with mocked DOM for every step in the wrapper file
- Step count / step ID assertions in wrapper tests unless they catch real regressions

**If you keep wrapper smoke tests**, prefer one representative step action + setup/cleanup wired — not seven duplicated DOM fixtures.

---

## 10. `estimatedMinutes` Must Reflect Actual Step Count

Re-calculate after any restructure. Rough guide: each step contributes ~25–35 seconds
at 1× speed (reading pause + action delays). Round up to the nearest minute.

- 6 steps ≈ 3 min
- 9 steps ≈ 4 min
- 12 steps ≈ 5 min

Update the lesson card in the domain list too — it displays `estimatedMinutes`.

---

## 11. Visual Validation (Done Checklist Item 1)

Section 10 is folded into **done checklist item 1** — see
[`docs/guides/demo-lesson-done-checklist.md`](../../docs/guides/demo-lesson-done-checklist.md).

Minimum before marking done:

1. Demo Hub → lesson → **Restart** → auto-play **all steps** at 1×
2. Final `verify` outcome visible
3. **Rapid Next** through stateful steps — `preAction` guards recover

Do **not** declare a lesson done without that manual pass. E2E smoke (checklist item 2) is the automated counterpart.
